===Tenure and political positions===

====Budget and economic issues====
[[File:Anthony Pratt and Kansas Governor.jpg|thumb|Kelly meeting with businessman [[Anthony Pratt (businessman)|Anthony Pratt]], October 2022.]]
Kelly was critical of the [[Kansas experiment]], the experimental Kansas budget of Governor [[Sam Brownback]] that led to cuts in schools, roads, and public safety. She would like to reverse those changes and pointed out that after there were major budget shortages she led a bipartisan effort to successfully balance the budget without increasing taxes.<ref name=":0"/><ref name=GOPAnger>{{Cite web|author-first1=John |author-last1=Hanna|url=https://apnews.com/ad172810a4b0dbabf6b2a56432d3dadf|title=Rising Kansas revenues fuel GOP anger over lack of tax cuts|work=Associated Press |date=February 3, 2020}}</ref>

In 2019, Kelly vetoed two Republican bills that would have cut [[state income tax]]es in Kansas.<ref name=GOPAnger/> She said that the state could not afford the cuts,<ref name=GOPAnger/> and that the Republican bill, which would have cut revenues by an estimated $245 million over a three-year period, would have precipitated a "senseless fiscal crisis" and created a budget deficit.<ref>{{Cite web|author-first1=Dion |author-last1=Lefler|url=https://www.kansas.com/news/politics-government/article230530174.html|title=Gov. Kelly vetoes Republican tax bill, says it would bring 'senseless fiscal crisis'|work=Wichita Eagle|date=May 17, 2019}}</ref> Kelly's decision, as well as higher-than-expected state revenue intakes, led to the state beginning its 2020 budget year with $1.1 billion in cash reserves.<ref name=GOPAnger/> She sought to use some of the reserves to pay down debt and make payments to the state pension system.<ref>[https://www.usnews.com/news/best-states/kansas/articles/2020-01-16/kansas-governor-seeks-to-use-cash-reserves-to-pay-off-debt Kansas Governor Seeks to Use Cash Reserves to Pay off Debt], Associated Press (January 16, 2020).</ref> In May 2022, she signed a bill into law that will gradually reduce the [[sales tax]] rate on food before eliminating it in 2025.<ref name="McLean2022">{{cite web |last=McLean |first=Jim |title=You'll be paying a lower sales tax for groceries in Kansas soon, but it won't go to zero until 2025 |website=KCUR | date=May 11, 2022 |url=https://www.kcur.org/news/2022-05-11/youll-be-paying-a-lower-sales-tax-for-groceries-in-kansas-soon-but-it-wont-go-to-zero-until-2025 |access-date=February 1, 2023}}</ref><ref name="Finnerty2022">{{cite web |last=Finnerty |first=Katharine |title=Kansas Gov. Laura Kelly signs 'Axe the Food Tax' bill into law |website=KSHB 41 Kansas City News |date=May 11, 2022 |url=https://www.kshb.com/news/local-news/kansas-gov-laura-kelly-signs-axe-the-food-tax-bill-into-law |access-date=February 1, 2023}}</ref>

During the 2024 Kansas legislative session, Kelly vetoed multiple bills intended to cut taxes, including a bipartisan bill that passed the Kansas House unanimously, arguing that the bills were too generous to the wealthy and would create financial problems for the state within a few years.<ref>{{Cite web |author-last2=Smith |author-first1=Tim |author-last1=Carpenter|author-first2= Sherman |date=2024-04-05 |title=Kansas House derails governor-backed tax legislation overwhelmingly approved by Senate|url=https://kansasreflector.com/2024/04/04/kansas-senate-passes-governor-endorsed-tax-reform-bill-sends-measure-to-kansas-house/ |access-date=2024-06-16 |website=Kansas Reflector |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Carpenter |first=Tim |date=2024-05-09 |title=Gov. Laura Kelly affirms plan to veto bipartisan $2.3 billion, five-year tax reform bill • Kansas Reflector |url=https://kansasreflector.com/2024/05/09/gov-laura-kelly-affirms-plan-to-veto-bipartisan-2-3-billion-five-year-tax-reform-bill/ |access-date=2024-06-16 |website=Kansas Reflector |language=en-US}}</ref>

====COVID-19 responses====
{{Main|COVID-19 pandemic in Kansas}}

In response to the [[COVID-19 pandemic in the United States]], Kelly, like other governors, took steps to halt the spread of the [[Severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2|SARS-CoV-2]] virus, which causes [[coronavirus disease 2019]] (COVID-19). She declared a state of emergency on March 12, 2020, following the state's first COVID-19 death,<ref>{{Cite web|author-first1=John |author-last1=Hanna |author-first2= Heather |author-last2=Hollingsworth|url=https://apnews.com/61c63fc718f716b9194fb3e3304ffccc|title=Kansas reports COVID-19 death; governor declares emergency|work=Associated Press |date=May 2, 2020|access-date= May 28, 2023}}</ref> and issued a 60-day ban on public gatherings of 50 or more people and a moratorium on utility shutoffs on March 16.<ref>John Hanna, [https://apnews.com/90881e25d6e359716cd09bf4206ef6e0 Kansas bans gatherings of 50; Kansas City area goes further], Associated Press (March 16, 2020). Retrieved May 28, 2023.</ref> On March 18, citing the unprecedented crisis, Kelly directed the end to all in-person [[K–12]] classes for the remainder of the school year, making Kansas the first state to take that step.<ref>{{Cite web|author-first1=Colin |author-last1=Dwyer|url=https://www.npr.org/sections/Coronavirus%20Live%20Updates/2020/03/18/817886198/kansas-becomes-the-first-state-to-end-its-school-year|title=Kansas Becomes The First State To End In-Person Classes For The Year|work=NPR |date=March 18, 2020}}</ref> On March 23, to combat the virus's spread, she limited public gatherings to ten people.<ref>{{Cite web|author-first1=Nicole |author-last1=Asbury|url=https://www.kansascity.com/news/coronavirus/article241437841.html|title=Kelly restricts public gatherings to no more than 10 to combat coronavirus spread|work=Kansas City Star|date=March 23, 2020|access-date= May 28, 2023}}</ref> On March 28, amid increasing deaths and illnesses, Kelly issued a "stay at home" order that directed all residents to remain at home, except for travel for essential work, essential business (such as traveling to obtain medical care or groceries), and outdoor exercise with [[social distancing]] measures.<ref name=StayHome>[https://apnews.com/425a488fcf8f16dfb0d7e06a9293b1ff Kansas governor orders residents to stay home to curb virus], Associated Press (March 28, 2020).</ref> Almost two dozen other states had already issued similar orders, and almost 75% of Kansas's population was already affected by similar orders from local officials, since 25 Kansas counties, including the most populous ones, already had stay-at-home orders in place.<ref name=StayHome/> Kelly strongly criticized the [[First presidency of Donald Trump|Trump administration]]'s slow response to the crisis and the federal failure to provide Kansas and other states with adequate supplies of [[personal protective equipment]] (such as masks, gloves, and gowns) and [[COVID-19 testing|testing kits]].<ref>{{Cite web|author-first1=John |author-last1=Hanna |author-first2= Heather |author-last2=Hollingsworth|url=https://apnews.com/aeaaea98338f18cc264ca23e694a4d6d|title=Kansas governor calls US officials unprepared for COVID-19|work=Associated Press|date=April 2, 2020|access-date= May 29, 2023}}</ref>

[[The Kansas City Star]]'s editorial board criticized Kelly's rollout of the COVID-19 vaccine, writing, "Kansas seems to be uniquely underperforming, a recurring issue for Kelly and the administration she leads." But the board added that Kelly did not bear all the burden for the state's response shortcomings because Republican state legislators were insisting that individual counties exercise sole authority with respect to COVID-19 efforts. That resulted in a slapdash response when treatment and vaccinations became available. If the state was not sufficiently prepared for the pandemic, many counties were even less so, citing [[Centers for Disease Control and Prevention|CDC]] data showing Kansas near the bottom of vaccinations per 100,000 residents.<ref>{{Cite web|date=January 8, 2021|title=Gov. Laura Kelly knew the COVID vaccine was coming. Why wasn't Kansas better prepared?|url=https://www.kansascity.com/opinion/editorials/article248363185.html|website=Kansas City Star}}</ref> Members of the Kansas congressional delegation, including [[Sharice Davids]], called on Kelly to do more to address the backlog of claimants at the [[Kansas Department of Labor|Department of Labor]] seeking unemployment payments from federal programs aimed to help residents out of work due to the pandemic.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Bahl|first=Andrew|title=Gov. Laura Kelly announces KDOL changes as sweeping modernization effort moves on|url=https://www.cjonline.com/story/news/state/2021/03/03/gov-laura-kelly-announces-changes-kansas-department-labor/6913590002/|access-date=2021-03-11|website=The Topeka Capital-Journal|language=en-US}}</ref>

On November 16, 2020, Kelly renewed her call for the legislature to join her in the issuance of masking orders.<ref>[https://www.kansas.com/news/coronavirus/article246621418.html Kansas Governor Laura Kelly renews push for mask mandate], ''[[Wichita Eagle]]'', Laura Kelly, via Facebook, October 21, 2020.</ref>

====Executive order on religious gatherings====
Because Kelly's orders on public gatherings applied to Easter Sunday celebrations in churches, the Republican-majority Legislative Coordinating Council (LCC),{{refn|group=nb|The LCC, established under Chapter 46, Kansas Statutes,<ref>[https://web.archive.org/web/20210916064400/https://casetext.com/statute/kansas-statutes/chapter-46-legislature/article-12-legislative-coordinating-council Chapter 46, Kansas Statutes]</ref> consists of the presiding officers and party leaders of both houses of the Legislature.}} a group of leaders of the [[Kansas Legislature]], voted to revoke her order on a 5&ndash;2 party-line vote on April 9, 2020, asserting that the order violated the [[free exercise of religion]].<ref name=church>{{Cite web|url=https://www.kansas.com/news/politics-government/article241861126.html|title=War over Easter: Kansas lawmakers revoke Gov. Kelly’s order limiting church gatherings|website=[[Wichita Eagle]]|author-first1=Jonathan|author-last1=Shorman|author-first2=Amy Renee|author-last2=Leiker|author-first3=Michael|author-last3=Stavola|date=April 8, 2020|access-date=April 9, 2020}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|author-first1=Sherman |author-last1=Smith|url=https://www.leavenworthtimes.com/news/20200411/kansas-coronavirus-update-supreme-court-sides-with-gov-laura-kelly-in-fight-over-church-crowds|title=Kansas coronavirus update: Supreme Court sides with Gov. Laura Kelly in fight over church crowds|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210415233800/https://www.leavenworthtimes.com/news/20200411/kansas-coronavirus-update-supreme-court-sides-with-gov-laura-kelly-in-fight-over-church-crowds|archive-date=April 15, 2021|work=Leavenworth Times|date=April 11, 2020|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref name=GovBlasts>{{Cite web|author-first1=Conner |author-last1=Mitchell|url=https://www2.ljworld.com/news/2020/apr/08/kelly-norman-blast-lawmakers-vote-to-rescind-order-limiting-in-person-religious-gatherings/|title=Governor blasts Kansas lawmakers' vote to rescind limits on in-person religious gatherings|work=Lawrence Journal-World|date=April 8, 2020}}</ref> Republican Attorney General [[Derek Schmidt]] opposed Kelly's order, issuing a memorandum calling it a violation of the Kansas state law, and urged law enforcement not to enforce it.<ref name=church/> Kelly called this "shockingly irresponsible";<ref name="church" /> at the time, there had been more than a thousand confirmed COVID-19 cases, and dozens of confirmed COVID-19 deaths, in Kansas,<ref name="GovBlasts" /> and of 11 identified sources of contagion, three had come from recent religious gatherings.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.kansas.com/news/coronavirus/article241810656.html|title=Kansas has 3 church-related COVID-19 clusters, state says amid scramble for supplies|website=[[Wichita Eagle]]|author-first1=Jonathan |author-last1=Shorman|date=April 6, 2020|access-date=April 9, 2020}}</ref> She challenged the LCC's decision in the [[Kansas Supreme Court]]. Following an expedited [[oral argument]] (conducted remotely via [[Zoom Video Communications|Zoom]] teleconference), the state Supreme Court unanimously reinstated Kelly's orders, concluding that her executive order was valid and that the LCC lacked the authority to overturn it.<ref>''[https://www.kscourts.org/KSCourts/media/KsCourts/High%20Interest%20Cases/Kelly%20v%20LCC/122765Opinion.pdf Kelly v. Legislative Coordinating Counsel] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220401172749/https://www.kscourts.org/KSCourts/media/KsCourts/High%20Interest%20Cases/Kelly%20v%20LCC/122765Opinion.pdf |date=April 1, 2022 }}'', Kansas Supreme Court (April 11, 2020).</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.kmbc.com/article/kansas-supreme-court-says-executive-order-banning-religious-service-of-more-than-10-people-stands-governor-laura-kelly-covid-19-coronavirus/32114710#|title=Kansas Supreme Court says executive order banning religious service of more than 10 people stands|work=[[KMBC-TV|KMBC]]|date=April 11, 2020|access-date=April 12, 2020}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|author-first1=Jason |author-last1=Breslow|url=https://www.npr.org/sections/coronavirus-live-updates/2020/04/12/832644243/kansas-supreme-court-upholds-governors-order-limiting-the-size-of-easter-service|title=Kansas Supreme Court Upholds Governor's Order Limiting The Size Of Easter Services|work=NPR |date=April 12, 2020}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|author-first1=Conner |author-last1=Mitchell|url=https://www2.ljworld.com/news/2020/apr/11/kansas-supreme-court-hears-arguments-in-governors-suit-against-lawmakers-decision-could-come-saturday/|title=Kansas Supreme Court says legislative panel didn't have authority to revoke governor's order|work=Lawrence Journal-World|date=April 11, 2020}}</ref>

A week later, in a separate case, U.S. District Judge [[John W. Broomes]] in [[Wichita, Kansas|Wichita]] issued a temporary [[restraining order]] blocking enforcement of Kelly's order as to two churches (one in [[Junction City, Kansas|Junction City]], the other in [[Dodge City, Kansas|Dodge City]]), contending that the restriction violated religious freedom and free speech rights.<ref>{{Cite web|date=2020-04-18|title=Judge doubts Kansas COVID-19 rule, blocks it for 2 churches|url=https://apnews.com/article/2dc708f8d69d2671af2301d086043ccc|access-date=2021-02-13|website=AP NEWS|author-first1=John|author-last1=Hanna}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Shorman|first=Jonathan|date=April 26, 2020|title=Kansas governor to issue less restrictive coronavirus rules beginning May 4|url=https://www.kansascity.com/news/politics-government/article242300191.html|access-date=|website=}}</ref> That case became [[Mootness|moot]] after Kelly issued a new executive order with less restrictive COVID-19 rules effective on May 4, 2020, under an agreement that allowed the churches to hold larger in-person services but required [[social distancing]].<ref name=StarkShorman>{{Cite web|author-first1=Carylynn |author-last1=Stark |author-first2= Jonathan |author-last2=Shorman|url=https://www.kansascity.com/news/politics-government/article242300191.html|title=Kansas governor to issue less restrictive coronavirus rules beginning May 4|website=Kansas City Star|date=April 25, 2020}}</ref>

====Healthcare====
As governor, Kelly pushed the Republican-controlled [[Kansas Legislature|Kansas legislature]] to accept the [[Medicaid expansion]] under the [[Affordable Care Act]],<ref>{{Cite web|author-first1=Phil|author-last1=McCausland|url=https://www.nbcnews.com/news/us-news/kansas-governor-top-republican-reach-deal-expand-medicaid-n1113071|title=Kansas governor, top Republican reach deal to expand Medicaid|work=NBC News |date=January 9, 2020}}</ref> to provide health care coverage to up to 150,000 Kansans.<ref name=HannaAP>{{Cite web|author-first1=John |author-last1=Hanna|url=https://apnews.com/f5cf2666dc8dbd4b88e1f313e28a655d|title=New Kansas proposal breaks impasse on expanding Medicaid|work=Associated Press |date=January 9, 2020}}</ref> A Medicaid expansion plan had passed the Kansas Legislature in 2017, but Brownback vetoed it.<ref name=HannaAP/> During every legislative session of her governorship, Kelly has proposed legislation that would expand Medicaid, but she has not yet succeeded. In 2025, the legislation is called the Healthcare Access for Working Kansans (HAWK) Act.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Kaminski |first=Anna |date=February 6, 2025 |title=Kansas governor takes another swing at joining 41 states that have expanded Medicaid |url=https://kansasreflector.com/2025/02/06/kansas-governor-takes-another-swing-at-joining-41-states-that-have-expanded-medicaid/ |access-date=February 12, 2025 |work=Kansas Reflector}}</ref>

Kelly has also supported reforming KanCare so that more citizens have access to health insurance.<ref name=":0">{{Cite news|url=https://www.ksn.com/news/your-local-election-hq/election-guide-laura-kelly-d-kansas-governor-/1527988448|title=Election Guide: Laura Kelly (D-Kansas Governor)|date=October 25, 2018|work=KSNW|access-date=October 25, 2018|archive-date=October 26, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181026071431/https://www.ksn.com/news/your-local-election-hq/election-guide-laura-kelly-d-kansas-governor-/1527988448|url-status=dead}}</ref>

====Human services====
Kelly proposed combining the Department of Children and Family Services with the Department of Aging and Disability Services into a consolidated, integrated Department of Human Services.<ref>{{Cite web|author-first1=Laura |author-last1=Howard|url=https://www.hutchnews.com/opinion/20200208/laura-howard-together-for-stronger-more-effective-human-services-agency|title=Together for stronger, more effective human services agency|work=Hutchison News|date=February 8, 2020|access-date=February 11, 2020}}</ref>

In January 2020, Kelly called for major changes to the [[Osawatomie State Hospital]], the long-troubled state psychiatric hospital that has faced scrutiny from federal regulators over security, safety, and treatment lapses. She has supported a plan for state funding for mental health crisis centers in the state.<ref>[https://apnews.com/5d86aa252dd3a641bfe9bddf25ef4ec3 Gov. Kelly: Kansas psychiatric hospital unit needs changes], Associated Press (January 3, 2020).</ref>

==== Immigrant rights ====
Kelly has clashed with immigrants rights advocates, signing a Republican-backed bill in 2022 that was written by her eventual gubernatorial opponent, then state [[Attorney General of Kansas|attorney general]] [[Derek Schmidt]]. The state law was written to prevent the implementation of a recently passed local law in [[Wyandotte County, Kansas|Wyandotte County]], which sought to enact a community identification card program intended to benefit immigrants, the formerly incarcerated, those experiencing homelessness, those aged out of the foster care system, and the elderly.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2022-04-12 |title=Kansas Governor Kelly signs 'sanctuary city' bill, dealing harsh blow to immigration activists |url=https://www.kcur.org/news/2022-04-11/kansas-governor-kelly-signs-sanctuary-city-bill-dealing-harsh-blow-to-immigration-activists |access-date=2024-06-16 |website=KCUR - Kansas City news and NPR |language=en}}</ref>

====School funding====
Kelly has largely reversed former governor [[Sam Brownback]]'s education agenda since taking office in 2019. She has said that she would like to ensure Kansas schools are funded and focus on improving Kansas students' performance to be competitive with other parts of the country. For example, she would address the statewide teacher shortage and improve pay for educators. She would also like to expand early childhood programs and increase options for students pursuing higher education.<ref name=":0" />

====Social issues====
In her first official act as governor, Kelly signed an executive order reinstating the [[LGBT employment discrimination in the United States|employment discrimination protections for LGBT state workers]] that Governor Sam Brownback had eliminated in 2015.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.kansas.com/news/politics-government/article224559230.html |title=Kelly reinstates protections for LGBT state workers in Kansas eliminated by Brownback |work=[[The Wichita Eagle]] |date=January 15, 2019 |access-date=May 16, 2019 |author-last1=Shorman|author-first1= Jonathan}}</ref>

Kelly opposed a Republican-proposed [[anti-abortion]] amendment to the [[Kansas Constitution]] in early 2020, saying it would return Kansas to the "[[Dark Ages (historiography)|dark ages]]". Amid acrimonious debate, the [[Kansas House of Representatives|state House]] fell four votes short of the two-thirds majority required to put it on the state ballot.<ref>{{Cite web|author-first1=Jonathan |author-last1=Shorman|url=https://www.kansas.com/news/politics-government/article240200447.html|title=Kelly accused of pressuring 'her own Catholic people' on abortion vote as tempers flare|work=Wichita Eagle|date=February 11, 2020}}</ref> In 2022, she expressed her opposition to the [[2022 Kansas abortion referendum]] that would have removed the right to an abortion from the state constitution. The amendment was defeated, with nearly 60% of voters rejecting it.<ref name="AP NEWS 2022">{{cite web |last=Hanna |first=John |title=Kansas governor lauds abortion vote but focuses on economy |website=AP |date=September 3, 2022 |url=https://apnews.com/article/abortion-2022-midterm-elections-voting-rights-health-d67531f2dd6856ac167ce48b3ec890ab |access-date=February 1, 2023}}</ref><ref name="NPR 2022">{{cite web |last1=Martinez |first1=A |last2=Kurtzleben |first2=Danielle  |title=Kansans vote to keep abortion legal in the state, reject constitutional amendment |website=NPR |date=August 3, 2022 |url=https://www.npr.org/2022/08/03/1115347126/kansans-vote-to-keep-abortion-legal-in-the-state-reject-constitutional-amendment |access-date=February 1, 2023}}</ref>

===Term limits and successor===
In November 2025—facing term limits that prevent her from running for reelection again—Kelly endorsed State Senator [[Ethan Corson]] for governor.<ref name= "endorsement_kansasreflector">Carpenter, Tim: [https://kansasreflector.com/2025/11/17/gov-laura-kelly-offers-formal-endorsement-of-democratic-sen-ethan-corson-for-governor/ "Gov. Laura Kelly offers formal endorsement of Democratic Sen. Ethan Corson for governor: Democratic leader favors candidate she labels 'true middle-of-the-road' politician"] November 17, 2025, ''Kansas Reflector'', retrieved July 21, 2026</ref><ref name= "only_one_kcstar">Kelly, Matthew: [https://www.kansascity.com/news/politics-government/article316449669.html "Kelly: Only one Democrat in race can win over 'extraordinarily moderate' KS voters,"] July 12, 2026, ''[[Kansas City Star]]'', retrieved July 21, 2026</ref>

==Personal life==
Kelly married physician [[Ted Daughety]], a specialist in pulmonary and sleep disorders, in 1983. They moved to [[Topeka, Kansas|Topeka]] in 1986. They have two adult daughters.<ref name="World" /> Kelly and Daughety divorced in 2024.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Carpenter |first1=Tim |title=Kansas Gov. Laura Kelly confirms divorce from spouse of more than 40 years |url=https://www.newsfromthestates.com/article/kansas-gov-laura-kelly-confirms-divorce-spouse-more-40-years |access-date=5 July 2025 |work=News From The States |date=July 3, 2025 |language=en}}</ref>

Kelly is Catholic.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://justfacts.votesmart.org/candidate/biography/34496/laura-kelly|title=The Voter's Self Defense System}}</ref>

==Other political activism==
In 2023, Kelly launched Middle of the Road PAC in an effort to elect candidates.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Carpenter |first=Tim |date=2023-08-07 |title=Gov. Laura Kelly forms PAC to support moderate GOP, Democratic legislative candidates |url=https://kansasreflector.com/2023/08/07/gov-laura-kelly-forms-pac-to-support-moderate-gop-democratic-legislative-candidates/ |access-date=2023-10-19 |website=Kansas Reflector |language=en-US}}</ref>

